# --- s018 Book I d.3 q.3 (= Ord. I d.3 p.1 q.3, nn. 167–201: the doubt about 'verum')
n("n359","s018-01","Tertia questio tertie distinctionis","la","commentary","There is no separate question here in the Vatican *Ordinatio*. The matter is the doubt within q. 3 at n. 167ff. (*quod 'verum' sit primum obiectum intellectus et non ens*) and its solution. Gorris (with B) treats it as a question in its own right. This must reflect the division of the *Ordinatio* text he used, which reaches the 'eleven questions' of d. 3 he counts at s016.","verified")
n("n360","s018-01","Veritas est rectitudo sola propria perceptibilis mente","la","source","Anselm, *De veritate* c. 11 (*veritas est rectitudo mente sola perceptibilis*), quoted by Scotus at *Ord.* I d. 3 n. 170. A's *propria* is not in Anselm and should probably be deleted, or read as *sola mente*.","verified")
n("n361","s018-01","ens et res prima impressione in anima imprimuntur","la","source","Avicenna, *Metaph.* I c. 5 (*res et ens… prima impressione imprimuntur in anima*), cited at *Ord.* I d. 3 n. 22.","verified")
n("n362","s018-02","opinio Egidii de roma","la","source","Giles of Rome, *Quodl.* IV q. 10 according to Gorris. The view that *verum* is the intellect's object is associated with Giles and with Henry. The reference has not been checked.","to-verify")
n("n363","s018-02","Aristo. 2°. de anima","la","source","Arist. *De anima* II 7, 418a29: the visible is colour, and colour is what lies on the surface of the visible *per se*.","certain")
n("n364","s018-03","non autem quod adequatur potentie in aliquo statu","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 186: *primum obiectum potentiae assignatur illud quod adaequatum est potentiae ex ratione potentiae, non autem quod adaequatur potentiae in aliquo statu*. Gorris reproduces the sentence and the candle example verbatim.","verified")
n("n365","s018-03","sub ratione infinitatis","la","commentary","Scotus's solution: being is the adequate object *de ratione potentiae*. The wayfarer's *de facto* limitation (*pro statu isto*) follows from sin or from the divine will (*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 187). Gorris reduces this to the claim that God is known *sub ratione entis* but not *ut tale ens*.","certain")
n("n366","s018-01","perceptible by the mind alone","en","translation","Following Anselm's wording rather than A's *sola propria … mente*.")
# --- B collation (full): B31L–B31R
n("n1085","s018-01","equalem communitatem","la","textual","B: *equalem concomitantem* (sic). A's *communitatem* is right: compare *Ord.* I d. 3 n. 167, *aeque commune*.","certain")
n("n1086","s018-03","illud quod adequatur visui","la","textual","B: *illud quod ponitur visui*.","certain")
n("n1087","s018-03","adequare visui ex se","la","textual","B: *adequari visui per se*.","certain")
n("n1088","s018-03","vel dico quod deus potest","la","textual","Before these words B adds *vel dico quod deus ens est obiectum et non sub ratione infinitatis*, a near-repetition of the preceding clause.","certain")
